centrates, solves problems, responds adequately. This suggests some reliability but not confirmed.\n\nWe can argue that D's lack of explicit reliability evidence, combined with moody/loner traits, makes him less suitable than C. Also note that C's reliability is explicit (100%). So we can reaffirm C.\n\nThus public message: I remain with Candidate C, because D's reliability is uncertain, and his moody/loner traits are concerning, while C's reliability is proven. Also note that B's communication issues remain a problem. So C remains best.\n\nWe need to include explicit facts: D takes responsibility seriously, can concentrate, solves problems extremely well, responds adequately to unexpected events (but no explicit reliability). C: 100% reliability, quick correct decisions, positive crew atmosphere. B: uncooperative, pretentious, nasty remarks. A: leadership but unfriendly, unorganized, unknown stress resilience.\n\nThus we can say: I still recommend C.\n\nProceed to output."}
